  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion id="AC1" testable="true">Creator closed set. The files under src/server that create a `sprk_communication` row are exactly CommunicationService.cs (outbound), EmailUploadCaptureService.cs, IncomingCommunicationProcessor.cs and MessagingIngestor.cs; those that create a `sprk_communicationattachment` row are exactly CommunicationService.cs, IncomingCommunicationProcessor.cs and MessageAttachmentMaterializer.cs; under Services/Communication and Services/Email the files that create a `sprk_document` row are exactly CommunicationService.cs, IncomingCommunicationProcessor.cs and MessageAttachmentMaterializer.cs, and the only caller of `CreateDocumentAsync` there is EmailAttachmentProcessor.cs. The ingestors' own counts are pinned (C1 1 communication; IncomingCommunicationProcessor 1 communication, 2 documents, 1 attachment row; C5 1 communication; C7 1 document, 1 attachment row; C6 1 CreateDocumentAsync). Negative: a new creating file, or an ingestor with an extra create, fails and names the file.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC2" testable="true">Owner calls per creator. C2 and C5 call, in order, `EvaluateAsync`, `OwnershipContextForNewRecordAsync`, `ResolveOwnerAsync` and (owned rows) `BuildNewRecordFieldsAsync` before the create, and call `ResolveOwnerAsync` exactly once in the file; every write of `ownerid` in those files is `new EntityReference("team", owner.OwningTeamId!.Value)` inside `if (owner.IsOwned)`. C3 and C4 apply the carried owner with `ApplyOwner` before each create and the file has no second `ResolveOwnerAsync`. C6 resolves before `CreateDocumentAsync` and returns without creating when the answer is not owned. C7 resolves before `UploadSmallAsync`, refuses on `IsRefused`, and applies the owner to both rows before their creates. Negative: dropping the resolve, creating before resolving, writing `ownerid` from any other source (a user, a message field, a literal), or re-resolving an attachment owner fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC3" testable="true">Dedupe on the message id. C2 and C5 create the communication only through `CreateCommunicationRaceProofAsync(communication, &lt;message&gt;.InternetMessageId, ct)` (no `CreateAsync(communication`), and their `if (wasDuplicate)` branch returns before the first later side effect (C2: cross-path link, association, thread, participants, attachments, .eml archive, enrichment; C5: association, thread, enrichment). C2 checks the Graph message id, then the canonical internet message id, then resolves the filing, then creates, in that order. `CreateCommunicationRaceProofAsync` in `DataverseServiceClientImpl` catches the alternate-key duplicate, re-reads the canonical row and returns `(existing.Id, true)`, and creates unguarded only for a blank id. `IncomingMessagingJobHandler` checks the idempotency key, takes the lock, re-checks, ingests, then marks, in that order, and `MessagingIngestor.IngestAsync` has one caller (the handler, through the dispatcher). Negative: a plain `CreateAsync` of the communication, a missing early return, a mark before the ingest, or a second caller of `IngestAsync` fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC4" testable="true">Unfiled exception on the ingestor path only. C1's source names no owner, owning-team, filing, stamp or thread-lookup column and no owner resolution (it is always unfiled); `RecordCreationService` still refuses a create on an `OwnershipException` table that is filed under nothing (both decision points); and exactly one registry rule carries an `OwnershipException` (the existing `RecordTableRulesTests` pin). Negative: C1 gaining `ownerid` or a regarding column, or a second creator of an unfiled communication outside the inventory, fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC5" testable="true">Attachment ownership through the same resolver. Every attachment, .eml and chat-attachment row an ingestor creates takes its owner from `IRecordOwnershipResolver` (carried `RecordOwnerResolution` or a `ResolveOwnerAsync` in the same method) and from nothing else; no ingestor file other than C2 and C5 writes `ownerid` at all. Negative: a row built with a literal or a user owner, or an attachment path that skips the resolver, fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC6" testable="true">No inbound principal. `RequestedBy` does not appear in `IncomingCommunicationProcessor`, `MessagingIngestor` or `EmailAttachmentProcessor`; in `EmailUploadCaptureService` it is set once from `RecordRequester.OfObjectId(userId)` where `userId` is a parameter never assigned in the file; in `MessageAttachmentMaterializer` it is set once from the request's `RequestedBy`. Negative: a message field (`From`, `Sender`, `envelope.`, `email.`) feeding `RequestedBy`, or a new `RequestedBy` in an inbound file, fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC7" testable="true">An attachment carries its parent link in the create (C6; added 2026-11-07 on the coordinator's instruction, replacing an earlier dead-path pin whose premise, a missing stamp, did not hold: a document has no stamp column and is rooted from its lookups). In `EmailAttachmentProcessor` the `CreateDocumentRequest` sets `ParentDocumentLookup` (null for an empty parent) and the association is applied to it with `DocumentAssociationMap.TryApply(createRequest, ...)` before `CreateDocumentAsync`; the `UpdateDocumentRequest` names no `ParentDocumentLookup` and no association lookup, and `TryApply` is never called on it; `DataverseServiceClientImpl.CreateDocumentAsync` stages both (`StageDocumentAssociation`, `StageDocumentParent`) and `StageDocumentParent` writes `sprk_parentdocument` as a lookup to `sprk_document`. Behaviour (data-mutation, fails first): the captured create holds the parent and the association for every accepted spelling, the captured update holds neither, an empty parent is no parent, an unsupported type is created unassociated with a warning, a refused owner creates nothing, a faulted create is never updated. Unit (fails first): the stager writes the lookup, writes nothing without a parent, and refuses an empty id. Negative: the parent link or association moving back to the update fails.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC8" testable="true">Controls and fail-first. Every rule predicate has at least one must-fire and one must-not-fire control (Theory or Fact). For each rule one mutation of the production source is run and the failing guard assertion is recorded in the task notes, with the restore confirmed (git diff of src is empty at the end unless a fix was made).</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC9" testable="true">Defect decision recorded. The task notes state, per creator, whether every owner its table carries is called. A gap found is either fixed with a test that fails first (and its production change measured per CLAUDE.md section 10) or reported with options and not built. Mapping into inbound rows is recorded as an owner question, not built.</criterion>
    <criterion id="AC10" testable="true">Verification. BFF build 0 errors and 0 warnings; the filtered BFF tests (`FullyQualifiedName~Communication|FullyQualifiedName~Email|FullyQualifiedName~Ingest|FullyQualifiedName~RecordOwnership`) pass with counts recorded; the ArchTests pass in full with the count recorded; the census differs from master by reason text only. No unfiltered `Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests` run.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Re-scoped 2026-11-07 (owner direction in fm's session, W3). Spec FR-21 part b says "create through the system principal"; under W3 its acceptance reads "each inbound creator is a named exception with a guard" (the spec text is updated in the main session, not here). Task 060's matrix names these creators and their exceptions. Task 033 (the other system creators) was dropped on the same rule. The original escalation (race-proof create option, unfiled owner rule, app-only mapper, carried owner) is closed by declining all four in R1; see notes/task-034-notes.md.</notes>

